# Getting Started with the Example Website

<!-- livebook:{"persist_outputs":true} -->

## Introduction

This Livebook walks through the **example-website** component, its architecture,
and how to interact with its APIs from Elixir.

The example-website provides a gRPC API (`example-grpc-api`) that you can
call using the `GRPC` library in Elixir.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:grpc, "~> 0.7"},
  {:protobuf, "~> 0.12"},
  {:jason, "~> 1.4"},
  {:req, "~> 0.4"}
])
```

## Defining the Proto Messages

First, let's define the protobuf messages that match our gRPC API:

```elixir
defmodule ExampleMessage do
  use Protobuf, protoc_gen_elixir_version: "0.12.0", syntax: :proto3

  field :example, 1, type: :string
end

defmodule Exampler.Stub do
  use GRPC.Stub, service: "Exampler"
end
```

## Calling the Service

Now we can connect to the gRPC service and make a request:

```elixir
# In a real scenario, you would connect to the actual service endpoint
{:ok, channel} = GRPC.Stub.connect("localhost:50051")

request = %ExampleMessage{example: "Hello from Livebook!"}

case Exampler.Stub.example(channel, request) do
  {:ok, reply} ->
    IO.puts("Response: #{reply.example}")

  {:error, reason} ->
    IO.puts("Error: #{inspect(reason)}")
end
```

## Health Check via HTTP

You can also verify the service is running via its HTTP endpoint:

```elixir
case Req.get("http://localhost:3000/health") do
  {:ok, %{status: 200, body: body}} ->
    IO.puts("Service is healthy: #{inspect(body)}")

  {:ok, %{status: status}} ->
    IO.puts("Unexpected status: #{status}")

  {:error, reason} ->
    IO.puts("Could not reach service: #{inspect(reason)}")
end
```

## Summary

| Endpoint | Protocol | Port |
|----------|----------|------|
| gRPC API | HTTP/2 | 50051 |
| Web UI | HTTP | 3000 |
| Health | HTTP | 3000 |

> **Tip:** Run this notebook in [Livebook](https://livebook.dev) connected to
> a running instance of the example-website for interactive exploration.
